TY  - CPAPER
AU  - Jbara, A
A1  - IEEE
TI  - Applying Model -Based Systems Engineering on CubeSat Budget Calculating
T2  - 2025 IEEE AEROSPACE CONFERENCE
CP  - 2025 Aerospace Conference-AERO-Annual
AB  - This work presents a tool based on Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) for automating the process of budget calculation mass, volume, power, and energy - for CubeSat nanosatellites based on user input which can be iteratively finetuned to fit the CubeSat limitations. CubeSat is a standard that defines nanosatellites with a base unit (1U) measuring 10x10x10 and a maximum weight of up to 2 kg per 1U. They are costeffective due to their use of commercial -off-the -shelf (COTS) components. This modularity provides flexibility on component selection to conform to the standard constraints that are forced by budget calculation. Modularity is fundamental in systems engineering design, which adopts a holistic approach to create systems by integrating various components and considering their interactions. CubeSat follows this approach. Object Process Methodology (OPM) and Systems Modeling Language (SysML) are popular modeling languages for systems engineering. This work benefits from MBSE for nanosatellite budget calculation. Specifically, it uses OPM to conceptually model it, applies OPM's computational capabilities on the produced model to calculate its budgets using OPM's coding capabilities, and eventually visually simulates the model by OPM's execution feature.
