TY  - JOUR
AU  - Bahthier, MTA
AU  - Harun, H
TI  - A Comparative Review and Methodological Framework for CubeSat Telemetry Downlink Architecture
T2  - JOURNAL OF AERONAUTICS ASTRONAUTICS AND AVIATION
AB  - The telemetry downlink subsystem plays a key role in maintaining reliable communication between CubeSats and their ground station. However, CubeSats operate under stringent constraints in power, bandwidth and processing capability, which often necessitate trade-offs in selecting modulation and coding schemes. This paper reviews telemetry downlink architectures implemented in recent CubeSat missions, examining each system in terms of modulation strategy, transmitter power, data rate, operating frequency, antenna configuration and mission type. The review shows that most CubeSat telemetry downlinks rely on static UHF configurations. While this approach supports reliability and implementation simplicity, it offers limited adaptability to the dynamic link conditions experienced during an orbital pass, potentially leading to underutilised link margin and reduced efficiency. More flexible downlink architectures are observed in some missions, but these are typically associated with payload data transmission or require system resources that exceed typical CubeSat constraints. In response to these observations, this paper proposes a telemetry downlink framework based on Variable Coded Modulation (VCM). The framework introduced adaptive MODCOD selection to better utilise link margin under time-varying channel conditions while maintaining reliability. It serves as a reference model for future validation and practical implementation in CubeSat telemetry systems.
